<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-undamped-mass-spring" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Undamped Mass-Spring System</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE + SPRING INTRO INTERACTIVE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->


<p>
  Alright, so we now have a full understanding of how to solve the ODE
  <me>ay''+by'+cy'=0. </me>
  This ODE turns out to have a very interesting application to the mass-spring model. 
  The idea behind the model is simple: a block of mass <m>m</m> is attached to a spring, 
  and we want to track its position over time.
     
</p>

<p>
  Press "Play" on the animation below to get a feel for how the mass moves.
  The mass oscillates back and forth between a compressed state and a stretched state,
  passing through the equilibrium position each time. The equilibrium position is simply
  where the spring sits at rest with no velocity: if the mass starts there undisturbed, it stays there.
  But if it starts displaced in either direction, the spring pulls it back, and the oscillation begins.
</p>
  <figure xml:id="fig-spring-intro">
    <caption>
      A horizontal mass-spring system. The block of mass <m>m</m> is attached
      to a spring with spring constant <m>k</m> fixed to a wall. We measure
      position <m>x(t)</m> from the <term>equilibrium position</term> (where
      the spring is neither stretched nor compressed). When <m>x \gt 0</m> the
      spring is <term>stretched</term>; when <m>x \lt 0</m> the spring is
      <term>compressed</term>; when <m>x = 0</m> the mass is at equilibrium.
      Press Play to watch it oscillate!
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-spring-intro"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-spring-intro.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2">
      <description>
        An animated horizontal mass-spring system. A block labeled m slides
        left and right on a frictionless surface, attached to a spring fixed
        to a wall on the left. The block oscillates back and forth. Labels at
        the bottom show x less than 0 as compressed, x equals 0 as
        equilibrium, and x greater than 0 as stretched. Press Play to start
        the animation.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

 

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOOKE'S LAW
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p>Borrowing some facts from physics, we will give a brief derivation of how to obtain an ODE modelling the system. 
  Everything below follows from just Hooke's Law and Newton's second law of motion.
  Later on we will mostly be focusing on interpreting the behavior of solutions to the ODE and how it relates to the physical reality. </p>
  <fact xml:id="def-hookes-law">
    <title>Hooke's Law</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>restoring force</term> exerted by a spring is proportional
        to the displacement <m>x(t)</m> from equilibrium:
        <me>F_s = -kx,</me>
        where <m>k \gt 0</m> is the <term>spring constant</term>. The negative
        sign means the force always pulls the mass back toward equilibrium.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </fact>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NEWTON'S LAW + ARRIVING AT THE ODE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    By Newton's second law, the total force on the mass equals <m>ma</m>,
    where <m>a = x''(t)</m> is the acceleration. Since the only force acting
    on the mass is the spring force <m>F_s</m>, we have:
    <me>mx'' = F_s = -kx.</me>
    Rearranging gives us the <term>undamped mass-spring ODE</term>:
    <me>mx'' + kx = 0.</me>
    We can simplify this by dividing through by <m>m</m> and letting
    <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>:
    <me>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0.</me>
  </p>
 <p>
  Let's give a quick summary of this below.
 </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-natural-frequency">
    <title>Undamped Mass Spring System</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      The <term>undamped mass-spring ODE</term> is:
    <me>mx'' + kx = 0,</me>
    where <m>m</m> is the mass of the object on the spring and <m>k</m> is the spring constant in the system. Dividing through
    by <m>m</m>, we can write this as 
     <me>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0,</me>
        where the constant <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m> is called the
        <term>natural frequency</term> of the system. It depends only on the
        spring constant and the mass. 
      </p>
        <p>
      This ODE describes <term>free undamped motion</term>. It is
      <em>free</em> because there is no external driving force, and
      <em>undamped</em> because there is no friction or damping force
      slowing the mass down. 
    </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

 
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SOLUTION VIA XREF
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Notice that <m>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0</m> is exactly in the form we saw
    in <xref ref="obs-simple-harmonic-ode"/>. The general solution is:
    <me>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t).</me>
    This is called <term>simple harmonic motion</term>: the mass oscillates
    back and forth forever, with the shape of the solution determined by the
    initial conditions <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       REMARK: A cos(omega(t - delta)) FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <remark xml:id="remark-amplitude-form">
    <title>An Equivalent Form</title>
    <p>
      The solution <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m>
      can also be written as a single cosine function:
      <me>x(t) = A\cos(\omega_0(t - \delta)),</me>
      where <m>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}</m> is the <term>amplitude</term>
      (the maximum displacement from equilibrium) and <m>\delta</m> is a
      phase shift. We will skip the proof, but see the dropdown if you are curious.
    </p>
    <proof>
      <title>Why these two forms are equivalent (can skim)</title>
      <p>
        Using the cosine angle subtraction formula:
        <me>A\cos(\omega_0(t - \delta)) = A\cos(\omega_0 t)\cos(\delta) + A\sin(\omega_0 t)\sin(\delta).</me>
        Setting <m>c_1 = A\cos(\delta)</m> and <m>c_2 = A\sin(\delta)</m>, we
        get <m>c_1^2 + c_2^2 = A^2(\cos^2\delta + \sin^2\delta) = A^2</m>, so
        <m>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}</m>. The two forms are identical.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </remark>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FULL INTERACTIVE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    <!-- PROSE: now that we know the solution, let's see what it looks like.
         Adjust c1, c2, omega and watch both the spring and the position graph. -->
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-spring-full">
    <caption>
      Simple harmonic motion. The left panel shows the spring oscillating;
      the right panel traces the position <m>x(t)</m> over time. Adjust
      <m>c_1</m>, <m>c_2</m>, and <m>\omega_0</m> to see how the solution
      changes. Notice that when <m>x \gt 0</m> the spring is stretched, when
      <m>x = 0</m> the mass passes through equilibrium, and when <m>x \lt 0</m>
      the spring is compressed.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-spring-full"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-spring-animation.html"
      width="100%" aspect="3:2">
      <description>
        An interactive mass-spring animation with two panels. The left panel
        shows an animated spring and block. The right panel shows the position
        x of t graphed against time. A red dot traces the curve as the
        animation plays. Sliders control c1, c2, and omega naught. Press Play
        to start. The dashed gray curve shows the full solution; the blue
        curve is the portion traced so far.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
       <p>Okay, so we covered a lot of ground on this page! There was quite a bit of set-up with the physics, but we
   will start getting more familiar with all these terms as we work through more examples. Let's summarize below: </p>
  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The ODE:</alert> A mass <m>m</m> on a spring with constant
          <m>k</m> satisfies <m>mx'' + kx = 0</m>, or equivalently
          <m>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0</m> where <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Simple harmonic motion:</alert> The general solution is
          <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m>, or
          equivalently <m>A\cos(\omega_0(t - \delta))</m> with amplitude
          <m>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Free undamped:</alert> No external force, no friction.
          The mass oscillates forever. This changes when we add damping next week!
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

